<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">State Protection Authority</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1945">1945</a>–<a href="/years/1956">1956</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as ÁVH, Államvédelmi Hatóság</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The State Protection Authority, ÁVH, was the secret police of communist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, known as the ÁVO until 1950. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matyas-rakosi">Mátyás Rákosi</a> it carried out mass arrests, torture and the show trials of real and imagined opponents. Hated by the population, its members were targeted during the revolution of 1956, after which it was dissolved.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/state-protection-authority.jpg" alt="State Protection Authority" width="320" height="360" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thommy, Oppashi · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emblem_of_the_State_Protection_Authority.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The force began in 1945 as the Budapest Department of State Political Police. In 1946 it became the State Defence Department of the Hungarian State Police, the ÁVO, and in 1950 the State Protection Authority. Gábor Péter headed it from 1945 to 1952. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laszlo-rajk">László Rajk</a>, the Communist minister of the interior, played a crucial part in organising it, and in 1949 became one of its victims: he was arrested, tried for nationalism and Titoism and executed. On 1 January 1950 the authority was removed from the Ministry of the Interior, made independent and given the Border Guard.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The authority served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungarian-working-peoples-party">Hungarian Working People's Party</a> directly, with little reference to the norms of government, and its actions were not subject to judicial review until the early years after Stalin. It gathered intelligence through a vast network of informants. Secret arrests were followed by periods of torture lasting from 3 to 18 months, after which the public prosecutors and courts were called in to pass sentence.</p><p>Prisons and internment camps were placed under its supervision, the most notorious at Recsk, Kistarcsa, Tiszalök and Kazincbarcika. Before 1953 some camps aimed at the eventual death of their inmates through overwork and maltreatment. The first government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imre-nagy">Imre Nagy</a>, from 1953 to 1955, greatly improved conditions in the camps and ended these efforts.</p><p>In 1953 the authority prepared a show trial meant to prove that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raoul-wallenberg">Raoul Wallenberg</a> had not been taken to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in 1945 but had been killed by Zionists in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a>. Leaders of the Jewish community, among them Miksa Domonkos and Lajos Stöckler, and two intended witnesses were seized and tortured. After the death of Stalin the preparations were stopped and the prisoners released in the autumn of 1953. Domonkos died shortly afterwards, mainly from the torture.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>On 29 October 1956, during the revolution, the militia of József Dudás stormed the secret police headquarters and massacred the officers inside, and the crowd lynched others. The students' and workers' councils then set up armed patrols to detain members of the authority for their own safety and for later trial. When Soviet forces intervened, its officers carried out brutal reprisals against revolutionaries.</p><p>The government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janos-kadar">János Kádár</a>, who had himself been tortured by the authority, did not revive it under its old name. Its work passed to a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of the Ministry of the Interior, known as III, which operated with considerably more restraint. Its former headquarters at 60 Andrássy Avenue in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a> is now a museum, the House of Terror.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “State Protection Authority” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mátyás Rákosi, leader of the Hungarian Working People's Party, ruled as a Stalinist dictator, with show trials such as that of László Rajk and a feared secret police, the <b>ÁVH</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/hungarian-peoples-republic">Hungarian People's Republic</a></li></ul></section>
